  "narrative": "No assessments are due. Because independent validation is exhausted, this exploration focuses on precision, technical robustness, co-alteration specificity, and heterogeneity around the strongest supported supplied-dataset dependency axes rather than broad driver screening. The largest supported associations are MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency, SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency, KEAP1 loss with weaker/less negative NFE2L2 dependency, PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency, CDKN2A loss with stronger MDM2 dependency, and TP53 loss with weaker/less negative USP7 dependency. I registered new mean-difference contexts that require both high screen quality (screen_roc_auc >= 0.95) and high Cas9 activity (cas9_activity_pct >= 80), sometimes also restricting to CDKN2A-intact lines. These filters test whether the effects persist when lower-quality screens and low Cas9 activity are removed and, for several axes, when a potentially confounding co-alteration is absent. The expected raw contrasts are: TP53-loss USP7 positive and KEAP1-loss NFE2L2 positive because these exposures are associated with higher, less negative dependency scores; MSI-high WRN, SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2, PTEN-loss STAT3, and the accepted direction for these comparisons negative because these exposures are associated with more negative, stronger dependency scores. Approximate 10-percent-of-absolute-mean thresholds are USP7 ~0.018, NFE2L2 ~0.009, STAT3 ~0.028, WRN ~0.030, and SMARCA2 ~0.042. I also registered a USP7 TP53-by-CDKN2A interaction in high-Cas9 lines, anticipating near zero heterogeneity because CDKN2A-intact and CDKN2A-loss mean-difference estimates have been similar, but this remains unresolved because formal interaction testing is useful. A PTEN-loss STAT3 interaction by lineage_subtype tests whether the PTEN effect is restricted to adenocarcinoma or extends to the non-adenocarcinoma subtype within this lung dataset. An EGFR amplification claim was added in the opposite direction for EGFR dependency in ERBB2-intact, TP53-intact, high-Cas9 lines because earlier ERBB2/TP53-intact evidence produced a borderline reverse signal, i.e. weaker EGFR dependency with amplification rather than stronger dependency. This is not a causal claim; it tests whether that borderline opposite signal survives a cleaner technical context. Finally, a USP7-by-screen_batch interaction was registered to assess whether batch_a differs from other screen batches, addressing possible technical confounding of the large TP53-USP7 association. All proposals are dataset-specific associations in the supplied lung CRISPR dataset and do not establish causality, therapeutic vulnerability, or external generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 12 explore update. Validation budget remains exhausted; no validation requested. Current accepted dataset-specific dependency axes: MSI-high stronger WRN; SMARCA4 loss stronger SMARCA2, robust including ARID1A co-strata and clean co-alteration subsets; KEAP1 loss weaker/less-negative NFE2L2, very large effect; PTEN loss stronger STAT3; CDKN2A loss stronger MDM2 globally and in many robust contexts; TP53 loss weaker/less-negative USP7, large and robust to screen quality, Cas9 activity, CDKN2A strata mean differences. Main unresolved issues: exact magnitude/interaction of TP53 by CDKN2A for USP7, TP53-loss-specific CDKN2A-MDM2 precision in very clean contexts, PTEN-STAT3 subgroup heterogeneity, EGFR amplification borderline reverse signal, and technical/batch modifiers. No assessments were due. New proposals target: (1) USP7 TP53 effect under combined high screen quality and high Cas9; (2) USP7 TP53-by-CDKN2A interaction among high-Cas9 lines; (3) PTEN-STAT3 under combined high quality/high Cas9/CDKN2A-intact; (4-6) WRN-MSI, SMARCA2-SMARCA4, NFE2L2-KEAP1 in combined high quality/high Cas9/CDKN2A-intact; (7) PTEN-STAT3 interaction by lineage subtype; (8) EGFR amplification opposite/weaker EGFR dependency in ERBB2-intact, TP53-intact, high-Cas9; (9) USP7 TP53-by-screen_batch interaction. These should be interpreted only as associations within the supplied dataset. For interactions, accept only if the signed heterogeneity estimate is both statistically clear and scientifically meaningful relative to the relevant outcome threshold; otherwise retain as null/uncertain. Broad driver-target hypotheses outside the accepted axes remain largely unsupported: BRAF V600E-BRAF, EGFR amplification global EGFR, ERBB2 amplification-ERBB2, KRAS mutation-KRAS, NF1 loss-RIT1, STK11 loss-KRAS, PTEN loss-KRAS, broad MYC amplification effects, initial TP53-loss-MDM2, research marker D/E/F targeted tests, and CDKN2A cross-effects on non-MDM2 outcomes. The next analyses should continue discovery precision/context and interaction testing, prioritizing technical quality, co-alteration specificity, and heterogeneity around the accepted axes."
